{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LKpV9keeo3Kf14S6p4TQgM","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on the files in the context filesystem, I can provide you with a clear answer:\\\\n\\\\n**No, a ThreadType field or parameter was NOT added to the error handler context.**\\\\n\\\\nHere's what the documentation shows:\\\\n\\\\nIn the discussion regarding KIP-1270 (extending ProcessingExceptionHandler to GlobalKTable processors), the question was raised about whether handlers needed a way to distinguish whether an error originated from a globalThread or a StreamsThread. \\\\n\\\\nInitially, in their proposal, Arpit Goyal suggested: \\\\\\\"We may need to introduce ThreadType(Stream or Global) in errorHandlerContext with ThreadType information\\\\\\\" (as stated in `_intake/2026/01/23/mail-bd0baeb6576bc00102b33e78-9ba1febc.md`, lines 66-67).\\\\n\\\\nHowever, **this approach was rejected**. Matthias J. Sax responded in `_intake/2026/01/27/mail-0cd1da9b3ea5d6d07a9cf457-dc90df48.md` stating:\\\\n\\\\n> \\\\\\\"I don't think we would need multiple handlers. The handler is invoked passing in `ErrorHandlerContext` parameter, which provides enough information to distinguish the case (eg, topic(), processorNodeId(), and taskId()), so users can implement different logic inside the same handler for the different cases if necessary.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nArpit Goyal then confirmed this decision in `_intake/2026/01/27/mail-7e2de452e42af04db5c25b47-b2e6e212.md` (line 29):\\\\n\\\\n> \\\\\\\"There is no need to introduce ThreadType parameter or another configuration for the same.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n**Sources:**\\\\n- `_intake/2026/01/23/mail-bd0baeb6576bc00102b33e78-9ba1febc.md`\\\\n- `_intake/2026/01/27/mail-0cd1da9b3ea5d6d07a9cf457-dc90df48.md`\\\\n- `_intake/2026/01/27/mail-7e2de452e42af04db5c25b47-b2e6e212.md`\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"store\\\",\\\"_intake/2026/01/23/mail-bd0baeb6576bc00102b33e78-9b